  "correct": "E",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Do not choose the right-angle vertex in advance",
      "body": "<p>Name the vertices and consider each possible location of the right angle. Write the relevant two side gradients in each case.</p>",
      "recap": "An unspecified right angle can occur at any vertex.",
      "pitfall": "A sketch of one plausible triangle does not exhaust the possibilities.",
      "pause": "List the separate vertex cases and check whether any side is vertical.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b2-l06",
          "reminder": "An unspecified right angle can occur at any vertex."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Impose perpendicularity in each case",
      "body": "<p>For the nonvertical perpendicular sides, set the product of their gradients equal to minus one. Solve every resulting equation.</p>",
      "recap": "Perpendicular finite nonzero gradients are negative reciprocals.",
      "pitfall": "When only the sum is requested, you may use a quadratic root sum, but must still check feasibility and duplicates.",
      "pause": "Check that each result gives a genuine triangle, then sum all distinct permitted k values.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-straight-lines",
          "reminder": "Perpendicular finite nonzero gradients are negative reciprocals."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1. Name the points.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The three segment gradients are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>. Their horizontal separations are nonzero.</p><p><strong>2. Put the right angle at A or B.</strong> At A, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>33</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>. At B, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3. Put the right angle at C.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>4. Check and add.</strong> All four cases give distinct nondegenerate triangles. Their sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>33</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2.25</mn></mrow></math>. <strong>Answer E.</strong></p>",
